<TopicPage slug="subjects/gender-studies/foundational/sex-gender-and-sexuality-the-vocabulary">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Gender studies begins by taking apart words everyone uses daily and assumes everyone means the
    same thing by. In ordinary English, "sex", "gender" and "sexuality" blur into one another —
    forms ask about "gender" when they want a tick-box, people say "gender" when they mean
    chromosomes, "orientation" when they mean identity. The field's first move is analytic: pull
    these apart into distinct concepts, not to win an argument by definition, but because the
    distinctions make sharper questions possible. Which differences between people track bodies?
    Which track societies? Which track desire? You cannot begin to answer until the concepts are
    separated — in the same way that physics could not begin until it separated weight from mass.
  </p>
  <p>
    An everyday analogy shows why this matters. Consider the difference between a biological fact
    (humans need roughly eight hours of sleep), a social norm (sleeping at night, in a bed, alone,
    is "normal" while napping at your desk is "unprofessional"), and an identity (the "morning
    person" versus the "night owl"). The biological fact is real; the norm varies across cultures
    and eras — siesta cultures, shift workers, medieval segmented sleep — and it changes what the
    fact <em>means</em> for your life. Nobody confused the biology with the norm would conclude
    that night-shift nurses are violating nature. Gender studies makes the same separation for
    one of the deepest sets of norms humans live by, and then asks what the norms are doing, whom
    they serve, whom they cost, and how they change.
  </p>
  <p>
    This page builds the working vocabulary in three passes — the sex/gender distinction,
    sexuality and identity terms, and the field's central theoretical debate, social construction
    versus essentialism — then shows the vocabulary doing real analytical work, and closes with
    the debates at the field's current edge.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the foundational distinctions" widget="equationStep" config={vocabCfg} />

  <h2>Distinguishing sex and gender</h2>
  <p>
    In the field's working vocabulary, <strong>sex</strong> refers to the biological attributes:
    chromosomes, gonads, hormone profiles and anatomy. The textbook binary — typically
    <em>female</em>: two X chromosomes, ovaries, higher oestrogen; <em>male</em>: an X and a Y,
    testes, higher testosterone — describes the majority well. But the word "majority" is doing
    work in that sentence, and the field insists on noticing it: intersex variations exist, in
    which chromosomes, hormones or anatomy do not fit the standard binary pattern. How often
    depends entirely on definition — strict estimates (for instance, chromosomal variations
    requiring clinical attention) run well under one birth in a thousand, while broader counts
    that include milder hormonal variations reach around one in a hundred, comparable to being a
    natural redhead. The analytic point is not the exact figure; it is that biological sex is a
    distribution with tails, not a clean switch, which immediately complicates any claim built on
    "biology is always exactly binary".
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Gender</strong> is everything social built on that biological ground: the roles,
    expectations, norms, symbols and identities a society attaches to being seen as a woman, a man
    or something else. What counts as masculine or feminine is strikingly unstable. Pink was
    marketed for boys and blue for girls in early twentieth-century America — the switch came
    mid-century. Trousers were scandalous on women a century ago and are unremarkable now.
    Which jobs are "men's work" flips across cultures and decades: teaching and clerical work were
    male-dominated professions when they required literacy and authority, then became
    female-typed as wages and status fell. If the mapping from bodies to roles were natural law,
    it would not need this much maintenance — schools, advertising, law and family life all work
    continuously to produce and enforce it, which is itself evidence of how much is social.
  </p>
  <p>
    The distinction then splits into finer instruments. <strong>Gender identity</strong> is a
    person's internal sense of their own gender — for most people aligned with the sex assigned
    at birth (cisgender), for transgender people not. <strong>Gender expression</strong> is how
    one presents: dress, movement, voice — which may or may not match expectations for one's
    identity. <strong>Gender roles</strong> are society's scripts: who is expected to provide
    care, to initiate, to lead, to defer. A person can be a cisgender woman with a masculine
    expression, or a transgender man with a conventional one; the concepts are separate axes, not
    one variable. The distinction was introduced into the research vocabulary in the 1950s–60s
    (John Money's "gender role", Robert Stoller's <em>Sex and Gender</em>), initially in clinical
    contexts, and it has done its work ever since by letting researchers ask, for any observed
    difference, <em>which</em> variable explains it.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that "gender is a social construct" means bodies and biology are imaginary, or that the field denies physiological difference. The claim is narrower and harder to dismiss: bodies are real, but their <em>meanings</em> are made. No society disputes that pregnancy happens to female bodies; societies differ enormously on what a pregnant person may therefore be paid, whether she may study, drive, appear unveiled, or inherit — and those differences are the constructed part, and the part where inequality lives.</p>

  <h2>Sexuality and identity: a separate axis</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Sexuality</strong> — desire, attraction and orientation — is a distinct dimension
    from gender identity, and one of the field's earliest findings is that the two axes are
    statistically independent. Knowing that someone is a man says little about whether he is
    attracted to men, women or both; knowing someone is transgender says nothing necessary about
    their orientation; a trans woman attracted to women, a cisgender man attracted to men, and a
    lesbian all share a same-sex orientation while differing completely in gender identity.
    The vocabulary keeps the categories from bleeding into each other.
  </p>
  <p>
    Orientation terms are descriptive labels for patterns of attraction: <strong>heterosexual</strong>
    (a different gender), <strong>homosexual</strong> or gay/lesbian (the same gender),
    <strong>bisexual</strong> (more than one), <strong>pansexual</strong> (attraction regardless
    of gender), <strong>asexual</strong> (little or no sexual attraction — a distinct orientation,
    not a dysfunction). Two refinements matter analytically. First, researchers distinguish
    <em>attraction</em>, <em>behaviour</em> and <em>identity</em>: population surveys repeatedly
    find that the share of people reporting any same-sex attraction or behaviour exceeds the share
    who adopt an LGB identity, because identity is also a social and cultural act — it requires a
    vocabulary, a community and usually some safety. Second, the modern umbrella
    <strong>LGBTQ+</strong> (lesbian, gay, bisexual, transgender, queer, plus others) deliberately
    gathers people whose common ground is shared stigma rather than shared identity — which is
    precisely why the analytic distinctions stay necessary inside the coalition.
  </p>
  <p>
    The cross-cultural record is the strongest evidence that both gender and sexuality are
    socially organised. South Asian <em>hijra</em> communities are legally recognised as a third
    gender in India, Nepal and Bangladesh; Samoan <em>fa'afafine</em> are accepted as an
    integrated gender category; many Indigenous North American nations recognised (and the term
    <em>Two-Spirit</em>, adopted in 1990, now gathers) people who filled neither standard role.
    The existence of institutionalised non-binary genders is not a modern invention — it is a
    standing demonstration that "how many genders are there?" is a question societies answer
    differently, and that the binary answer is one cultural convention among several, not a
    census of nature.
  </p>

  <h2>Social construction versus essentialism: the field's core debate</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Essentialism</strong> is the position that categories like "woman" and "man" have a
    fixed, natural essence that determines their members' traits and destinies — "men are
    naturally competitive, women naturally nurturing" is the paradigm sentence. The field's
    dominant counter-position, <strong>social constructionism</strong>, holds that the meanings
    of gender categories are produced by social processes and therefore vary across cultures and
    change over time. Three qualifications keep the debate honest. Constructionism does not claim
    biology is irrelevant — hormones and physiology influence tendencies, and the field has
    accepted this increasingly since biologists like Anne Fausto-Sterling insisted that the real
    biology is itself more variable and interactive than the cartoon. It does not claim
    everything is chosen or infinitely malleable — individuals experience gender as deeply felt
    and often immovable. And it does not claim constructs are unreal: money is a social construct
    and remains entirely real in its effects. A construct, in the social-scientific sense, is
    something that exists <em>because</em> societies maintain it — which is exactly what makes it
    analysable, criticisable and changeable in a way that natural law is not.
  </p>
  <p>
    Judith Butler's <em>Gender Trouble</em> (1990) radicalised the position with the claim that
    gender is <em>performative</em>: not a fixed identity that expression merely reveals, but an
    identity continuously produced by the very repeated acts — dress, gesture, speech — that seem
    to express it, the way "standing" is produced by continuously standing rather than revealed
    by it. Judith Lorber's <em>Paradoxes of Gender</em> (1994) gives the sociological version: a
    society constantly "does gender" — in bathrooms, sports categories, names, dress — and then
    treats the resulting pattern as evidence of nature. The empirical work since has consistently
    favoured the constructionist account wherever the two make different predictions: roles vary
    across cultures (essentialism predicts constancy), gender-typing shifts within generations
    (essentialism predicts stability), and intersex and transgender experience shows biology
    itself refusing the tidy categories the essentialist argument requires.
  </p>
  <p>
    What the distinction buys is visible in one worked example: the gender pay gap. Take the
    widely cited US figure that full-time working women earn roughly 80–85 cents per dollar paid
    to men. Essentialism reads it as one fact about natural difference. Decomposition analysis —
    which is only possible once sex, gender roles and choice-sets are kept conceptually distinct —
    un-bundles it: part is hours and interruptions (gendered: caregiving still falls
    disproportionately on women); part is occupational segregation (which jobs are coded
    "women's" and paid less — a social pattern that flips when wages and status change); part is
    within-job pay differences (partly discrimination, partly negotiation structures); part is
    explained by measured factors. Each component has a different remedy — parental leave policy,
    pay-transparency law, promotion audit — and a politics that treats the gap as one
    undifferentiated fact of nature has, in effect, chosen to remedy none of them. The vocabulary
    is not pedantry; it is the difference between a question that can be answered and one that
    cannot.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three refinements carry the vocabulary toward intermediate analysis. <strong>Intersectionality</strong>
    (Kimberlé Crenshaw, 1989) observes that gender never operates alone: a Black woman, a
    working-class woman and a disabled woman experience "womanhood" through different
    interlocking structures, and single-axis analysis systematically misses those who sit at the
    crossings — Crenshaw's original cases were women of colour who fell between the protections
    of race discrimination law and sex discrimination law. <strong>Doing gender</strong> (West
    and Zimmerman, 1987) reframes gender as a routine social accomplishment — something people
    continuously <em>do</em> and are held accountable for, in every interaction, which explains
    the social cost of ambiguity and the policing of "gender nonconformity". And the biological
    nuance keeps deepening: endocrine and neuroscientific work increasingly studies variation
    within sexes rather than mean differences between them, while the concept of "sex assigned
    at birth" — used clinically long before it became politically contested — reminds us that
    even the assignment act is an interpretation made in a social world. The field's through-line
    since its founding is constant: gender is a lens on power — who gets to be what, who is
    entitled to what, and who pays for the maintenance of the categories — and its analysis is
    empirical as well as political. The vocabulary on this page is the entry fee to every
    conversation the field is currently having.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NWSA programme guidelines; Butler, <em>Gender Trouble</em>; Lorber, <em>Paradoxes of Gender</em>; Fausto-Sterling, <em>Sexing the Body</em>; Crenshaw, "Demarginalizing the Intersection of Race and Sex" (1989).</p>
